Here's a JavaScript function that takes an array of 5 numbers and checks if any of them is a valid prime number:

```javascript
function checkPrime(arr) {
  // Loop through the array
  for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
    // Get the current number
    const num = arr[i];

    // Check if the number is a prime
    if (isPrime(num)) {
      // If it's a prime, print it to the console
      console.log(num);
      // Break out of the loop since we found a prime
      break;
    }
  }

  // Helper function to check if a number is prime
  function isPrime(n) {
    if (n < 2) return false;
    for (let I = 2; I <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
      if (n % I === 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```

To use this function, simply pass an array of 5 numbers as an argument:

```javascript
const numbers = [2, 7, 15, 23, 29];
checkPrime(numbers); // Output: 2, 7, 23, or 29 (one of them)
``` 

Note that this function will only print the first prime number it finds in the array. If multiple primes are present, only the first one will be printed.

